---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title="What does optional: true actually change?" description="Practice card. One option flag, four candidate effects. Drills the validation surface of belongs_to.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Reading the Source · Card 1</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">What does <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-3xl">optional: true</code> actually change?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">One option flag separates these two models. The visible difference is "must have a user vs not." The mechanism behind it is more specific than that.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <Icon name="lucide:search" class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500 inline-block" aria-hidden="true" /> The code
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">Same association. Different behavior. What did Rails wire up differently?</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`class Post < ApplicationRecord
  belongs_to :user
end

class Comment < ApplicationRecord
  belongs_to :user, optional: true
end`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A Post with no user can't save. A Comment with no user saves fine. Which of these is the actual mechanism behind that difference?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'It tells the database to allow NULL in the foreign key column.',
              explanation: 'Whether the column allows NULL is decided by the migration (<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">null: false</code> or not). <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> never modifies your schema.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: 'It skips installing the presence validation that <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> would normally add.',
              explanation: 'Rails 5+ <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> installs a presence validation by default. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">optional: true</code> tells the builder to skip it. You can confirm with <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">Post.validators_on(:user)</code> vs <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">Comment.validators_on(:user)</code>.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'It makes the association polymorphic so the comment can belong to anything.',
              explanation: 'Polymorphism comes from <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">polymorphic: true</code>, which is a separate option. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">optional: true</code> doesn\'t change cardinality.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'It removes the helper methods like <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">build_user</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">create_user</code>.',
              explanation: 'The helper methods are generated regardless of <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">optional</code>. They come from the association itself, not from the validation.',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>Whether <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> installs a presence validation by default is controlled by the global setting <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">config.active_record.belongs_to_required_by_default</code>. In Rails 5+, that's <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">true</code> for new apps. In apps upgraded from Rails 4, it's often still <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">false</code>. Two Rails apps can run the same <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to :user</code> line and behave differently. The behavior isn't in the line; it's in the setting the line consults.</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/source-belongs-to"
          theoryLabel="Reading the Source · Card 1 — What belongs_to :user actually adds"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Back to Course</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-when-sql-runs" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 2 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
